prompts.text:
Build a small tower-style middleware stack in async Rust. Every file printed in full, path first. Rust 1.75, edition 2021, dependencies limited to tokio (with `full` and `test-util`), futures, pin-project-lite, and thiserror. Do not use the `tower` crate itself - you are writing the equivalent.

Files:
  Cargo.toml
  src/lib.rs
  src/service.rs
  src/retry.rs
  src/timeout.rs
  src/backoff.rs
  tests/retry_tests.rs
  tests/stack_tests.rs

src/service.rs defines the core abstraction:
  pub trait Service<Request> {
      type Response;
      type Error;
      type Future: Future<Output = Result<Self::Response, Self::Error>> + Send;
      fn poll_ready(&mut self, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Result<(), Self::Error>>;
      fn call(&mut self, req: Request) -> Self::Future;
  }
Also define `pub trait Layer<S> { type Service; fn layer(&self, inner: S) -> Self::Service; }` and a `ServiceBuilder` that composes layers in the order the user writes them (outermost first) - state the ordering explicitly in rustdoc and make the tests prove it.

src/backoff.rs: `pub trait Backoff { fn next_delay(&mut self, attempt: u32) -> Option<Duration>; }` with `ExponentialJittered { base, factor, max_delay, max_attempts, jitter: JitterKind }` where JitterKind is None, Full, or Equal. Jitter must be injectable for tests: parameterize on an `Rng` trait you define with a deterministic `SeededRng` implementation. `next_delay` returns None once attempts are exhausted.

src/retry.rs: `Retry<P, S>` where `P: Policy<Request, Response, Error>` with
  pub trait Policy<Req, Res, E>: Clone {
      fn retry(&mut self, req: &Req, result: Result<&Res, &E>) -> bool;
      fn clone_request(&self, req: &Req) -> Option<Req>;
  }
If clone_request returns None the request cannot be retried and the first result is returned as is. The retry future must be a hand-written `Future` implementation using pin-project-lite with an explicit state enum (Calling, Waiting, Done) - do not use `async fn` or `Box::pin` for the main future. This is the point of the exercise; a boxed future is a fail.

src/timeout.rs: `Timeout<S>` wrapping a service with a per-call deadline, producing a `TimeoutError` that composes with the inner error type via a `From` bound. Explain in a comment why the deadline must start in `call` and not in `poll_ready`.

tests/retry_tests.rs: use `tokio::time::pause()` and `advance()` so no test sleeps in real time. Cover: succeed on the third attempt with the exact expected total virtual time given base 100ms and factor 2.0 (compute it and assert it); max_attempts exhaustion returning the last error; a policy refusing to retry a 400-class error; clone_request returning None; and jitter with a seeded rng producing a reproducible delay sequence that you assert element by element.

tests/stack_tests.rs: build ServiceBuilder::new().layer(Timeout::layer(Duration::from_millis(250))).layer(Retry::layer(policy)).service(inner) and assert the ORDER of effects - specifically, whether the timeout applies to the whole retry sequence or to each attempt, given the composition order. Then build the reverse stack and assert the other behaviour. Both must be tested and the difference explained in a comment, because this is the thing everyone gets wrong.

Finish with a short analysis: where does your Retry future allocate, is it `Send` when the inner service is, and what would break if `Policy::clone_request` were allowed to return a different request than the original.
